  "id": "handlers",
  "title": "REST controllers",
  "summary": "A controller is a class in `server/controllers/` that answers HTTP requests. `RequestRoute` on the class gives its path, and `Public`, `SignedIn` or `Allow(Role)` says who may call it. `GetRoute`, `PostRoute` and the other route annotations mark the methods that answer. Each method name starts with its verb, such as `getProducts`. A returned model, or a list of models, becomes JSON with the model's field names. `ResponseStatus(201)` sets the status code. `FromBody` reads the JSON body into a model (a data type in `server/models/`). A body that does not fit the model gets 400 before the method runs. To set the status, headers and body yourself, answer with the language's own response: a Fetch `Response` in JavaScript and TypeScript, an `(HTTPStatus, headers, body)` tuple in Python, a `[status, headers, body]` array in Ruby, a `{ status, headers, body }` table in Lua, `http_response_code()`, `header()` and `echo` in PHP, the same `HttpExchange` in Java, Groovy and Kotlin, `HttpResponseMessage` in C# and F#, `http::Response` in Rust, a shelf `Response` in Dart and `*http.Response` in Go. If you return `text/html` yourself, Yon passes it through unchanged. Yon never renders it. A controller needs no framework import, base class, registration call or package. C++ has no tab here, because this example sets its status with `ResponseStatus`, which C++ route controllers do not have yet: the build refuses it in C++ with `TY2015` (ADR 0099).",
  "group": "Yon server",
